<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<template xmlns="http://schemas.openehr.org/v1">
    <id>
        <value>lifestyle_medicine_stress.v0</value>
    </id>
    <name>
        <value>Lifestyle Medicine Stress and Recovery Assessment</value>
    </name>
    <description>
        <original_author>
            <item>
                <key>name</key>
                <value>Ricardo Lourenco dos Santos</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>organisation</key>
                <value>Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto (FMUP)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>email</key>
                <value>ricardolourencosantos@gmail.com</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>date</key>
                <value>2026-01-29</value>
            </item>
        </original_author>
        <lifecycle_state>in_development</lifecycle_state>
        <details>
            <purpose>To record stress and recovery indicators from consumer wearable devices, combining HRV-based autonomic nervous system assessment with subjective wellness measures for lifestyle medicine practice.</purpose>
            <keywords>stress, recovery, readiness, HRV, autonomic nervous system, wellness, mental health, resilience, wearable</keywords>
            <use>Use for recording stress and recovery measurements including:
- HRV-based stress scores (proprietary algorithms)
- Recovery/readiness scores
- Autonomic balance indicators (LF/HF conceptual)
- Morning readiness assessment
- Training load vs recovery balance
- Subjective wellness inputs (mood, energy, soreness)
- Mindfulness session tracking
- Breathing exercise documentation

Primary use cases:
1. Daily morning readiness check
2. Pre/post exercise recovery assessment
3. Longitudinal stress pattern monitoring
4. Mind-body intervention tracking
5. Correlation of subjective and objective measures

Compatible devices: Oura Ring, Whoop, Garmin (Body Battery), Apple Watch (varies), Polar</use>
            <misuse>Not for:
- Clinical psychological assessment
- Diagnosis of anxiety or depression disorders
- Replacement for professional mental health evaluation</misuse>
            <copyright>CC BY-SA 4.0</copyright>
        </details>
        <other_details>
            <item>
                <key>licence</key>
                <value>Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>custodian_organisation</key>
                <value>FMUP - Faculty of Medicine, University of Porto</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>references</key>
                <value>1. Thayer JF, et al. A meta-analysis of heart rate variability and neuroimaging studies. Neurosci Biobehav Rev. 2012;36(2):747-756.
2. Kim HG, et al. Stress and Heart Rate Variability: A Meta-Analysis and Review. Psychiatry Investig. 2018;15(3):235-245.
3. Plews DJ, et al. Heart rate variability and training-intensity distribution in elite rowers. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2014;9(6):1026-32.
4. Kivimäki M, Steptoe A. Effects of stress on the development and progression of cardiovascular disease. Nat Rev Cardiol. 2018;15(4):215-229.</value>
            </item>
            <item>
                <key>FHIR_mapping</key>
                <value>Maps to StressRecoveryObservation profile in iOS Lifestyle Medicine FHIR IG. Uses HRV metrics as objective basis.</value>
            </item>
        </other_details>
    </description>
